{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom at head ce84c5b4: the bounded status is guarded too.\n\nSuccessor to 7d42a448, closing the remaining blocker against 78517753.\n\nReview found the surface test covered one renderer while the change claimed both. TestRenderedSurfacesNameEventsByNumber exercises workroom.RenderStatus; the bounded CLI status in internal/statusview is a separate renderer with its own rows, and swapping name() back to short() there left every test green.\n\nWhat makes this worth recording is how I missed it. I did run a mutation probe — that was the whole argument of the previous report — but I mutated render.go, watched the test fail, and took that as evidence for the change rather than for the file. Proving sensitivity on the surface already covered says nothing about the surface that is not. It is the same defect I reported against another head an hour earlier: a test that appears to walk a path and walks a different one.\n\nThe new test builds a projection whose decisions carry nonzero sequences, renders the bounded status, and asserts the commitment request and artifact event appear as #41 and #42 with neither appearing abbreviated — and that the git commit is still abbreviated, because that asymmetry is deliberate rather than an oversight.\n\nMutation-checked per renderer this time rather than per package: restoring short(item.Request) in renderCommitments fails it, and restoring short(item.Event) in renderArtifacts fails it, each independently.\n\nGates at this head: `go vet ./...` clean; the full `go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...` suite exits 0 with 15 packages passing; `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; `npm test` 69/69; `git diff --check` clean; clean worktree; merges cleanly with current main c0f3533d.","body":{"commit":"ce84c5b4a1c8614c2049047801291c645efd6d2d","path":"internal/workroom"}}